Summary
- Example Java code showing a safe, production-ready pattern to query a SQL database for a customer row by customer ID.
- Uses prepared statements (prevents SQL injection), try-with-resources, DataSource injection, and maps results to a Customer object.
- Includes a simple HikariCP DataSource setup, a DAO method, and a usage example.

Assumptions
- Customer ID is an integer (if yours is a string/UUID, see note below).
- Table name: customers. Columns: customer_id, first_name, last_name, email, created_at.
- Using JDBC + DataSource (HikariCP recommended) — works with MySQL/Postgres/SQL Server with the appropriate JDBC driver.

1) Customer model
```java
public class Customer {
    private final int customerId;
    private final String firstName;
    private final String lastName;
    private final String email;
    private final java.time.OffsetDateTime createdAt;

    public Customer(int customerId, String firstName, String lastName, String email, java.time.OffsetDateTime createdAt) {
        this.customerId = customerId;
        this.firstName = firstName;
        this.lastName = lastName;
        this.email = email;
        this.createdAt = createdAt;
    }

    // getters
    public int getCustomerId() { return customerId; }
    public String getFirstName() { return firstName; }
    public String getLastName() { return lastName; }
    public String getEmail() { return email; }
    public java.time.OffsetDateTime getCreatedAt() { return createdAt; }
}
```

2) DAO: getCustomerById using PreparedStatement and try-with-resources
```java
import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.sql.*;
import java.time.OffsetDateTime;
import java.time.ZoneOffset;
import java.util.Optional;

public class CustomerDao {
    private final DataSource dataSource;

    public CustomerDao(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    public Optional<Customer> getCustomerById(int customerId) throws SQLException {
        String sql = "SELECT customer_id, first_name, last_name, email, created_at FROM customers WHERE customer_id = ?";
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();
             PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {

            ps.setInt(1, customerId);

            try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
                if (rs.next()) {
                    int id = rs.getInt("customer_id");
                    String firstName = rs.getString("first_name");
                    String lastName = rs.getString("last_name");
                    String email = rs.getString("email");
                    Timestamp ts = rs.getTimestamp("created_at");
                    OffsetDateTime createdAt = ts != null ? ts.toInstant().atOffset(ZoneOffset.UTC) : null;

                    Customer customer = new Customer(id, firstName, lastName, email, createdAt);
                    return Optional.of(customer);
                } else {
                    return Optional.empty();
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

3) Example DataSource setup with HikariCP (quick example)
```java
import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariConfig;
import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;

public class DataSourceFactory {
    public static HikariDataSource create(String jdbcUrl, String username, String password) {
        HikariConfig cfg = new HikariConfig();
        cfg.setJdbcUrl(jdbcUrl);
        cfg.setUsername(username);
        cfg.setPassword(password);
        cfg.setMaximumPoolSize(10);
        cfg.setMinimumIdle(2);
        cfg.setPoolName("app-pool");
        // additional tuning...
        return new HikariDataSource(cfg);
    }
}
```

4) Usage example (main)
```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HikariDataSource ds = DataSourceFactory.create(
            "jdbc:postgresql://db-host:5432/mydb", "dbuser", "secret"
        );

        CustomerDao dao = new CustomerDao(ds);

        int customerId = 123; // input/param validated earlier
        try {
            Optional<Customer> maybeCustomer = dao.getCustomerById(customerId);
            maybeCustomer.ifPresentOrElse(
                c -> System.out.println("Found customer: " + c.getFirstName() + " " + c.getLastName()),
                () -> System.out.println("Customer not found")
            );
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            // log and handle - don't expose DB details to users
            e.printStackTrace();
        } finally {
            ds.close();
        }
    }
}
```

Notes & Best Practices
- Use PreparedStatement to prevent SQL injection; never concatenate user input into SQL.
- Validate customerId before calling (e.g., non-negative). If your ID is a UUID/string, use ps.setString(1, uuidString) and adjust parameter type.
- Prefer DataSource with connection pooling (HikariCP) for performance.
- Consider wrapping SQLExceptions in a runtime DataAccessException for simplified upper-layer error handling.
- If using Spring, prefer JdbcTemplate or Spring Data repositories to reduce boilerplate.
- If you want to return many fields or map relationships, use an ORM (Hibernate) or a lightweight mapper (JDBI, MyBatis).

If you want, I can:
- Provide a version using UUID (String) customer IDs.
- Show the same method with Spring JdbcTemplate or JPA/Hibernate.
- Add unit tests using an in-memory DB (H2) and a Docker-compose example for local testing. Which would you prefer?
